 ## Overview
-Early Ray Termination with Binary Mask is an acceleration technique for alpha-blending volume rendering that combines front-to-back compositing with a binary mask to skip fully transparent regions [S1, unofficial]. It terminates the ray evaluation when the accumulated transmittance drops below a predefined threshold, thereby ignoring the ray tail [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from sibling methods by its use of a pre-computed binary mask to jump directly to opaque voxels, avoiding the evaluation of empty space entirely, whereas siblings like [Empty-space skipping](1002-empty-space-skipping.md) may rely on hierarchical structures or [Conservative Early Ray Termination](1949-conservative-early-ray-termination.md) may use different termination criteria.
+An acceleration technique for alpha-blending that uses a binary volume to skip fully transparent cells and terminates the front-to-back evaluation when accumulated transparency falls below a threshold [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard early ray termination by explicitly encoding the presence of opaque voxels into a bit-mask structure, allowing the renderer to jump directly to non-empty regions rather than sampling every voxel along the ray [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- Volume data `volume[z][y][x]` containing color and opacity for each voxel.
-- Binary mask `mask[i][y][x]` where `i` indexes 32-voxel blocks along the Z-axis.
-- Image coordinates `x`, `y`.
-- Termination threshold `threshold` (transmittance value).
+- Volume data: A 3D array of voxels, where each voxel contains a color and an opacity value.
+- Binary mask: A 3D array of 32-bit integers, `mask[i][y][x]`, where `i` represents a block of 32 depth slices, and `y, x` are pixel coordinates. Each bit in the integer corresponds to a voxel in that block; a set bit indicates the voxel is opaque (or non-transparent), and a cleared bit indicates it is transparent.
+- Threshold: A pre-defined double value for accumulated transparency below which the ray terminates.
 
 **Outputs:**
-- Final pixel color `rgb`.
+- A single RGB color value for the pixel at `(x, y)`.
 
